(Compile and run Vox programs through the harness.

 Compiling goes through the shell, because the compiler's stderr must be
 captured to a file and Vox cannot redirect a child's output itself.
 Running NEVER goes through the shell: supervise reads the true wait
 status, which is the only way to tell a program that exits with code 139
 from one killed by signal 11.)

a text called vox_binary is "../vox/target/release/vox".
If the environment variable "VOX" exists then, Set vox_binary to environment's "VOX".
(Pin the compiler runtime: an installed /usr/share/vox/coreasm silently
 shadows the repo copy when VOX_CORE_PATH is unset, which would mean
 fuzzing the packaged compiler instead of the one under development.
 Declared, not Set: Set on an undeclared name conjures a number global
 holding the string's pointer - language note 13.)
a text called vox_core is "../vox/coreasm".
If the environment variable "VOX_CORE_PATH" exists then, Set vox_core to environment's "VOX_CORE_PATH".

To 'vox path'.
    Return a text, vox_binary.

(The compiler gets its own deadline, separate from --timeout: --timeout
 bounds how long a fuzzed *program* may run (harness.vox's supervise, via
 'run program'), and has never governed compilation. This budget is a
 vox-fuzz constant, not a CLI option - name it honestly so a -1 exit code
 from 'compile vox' reads as "the compiler itself hung" rather than being
 misread against the run timeout.)
a number called 'the compile budget in milliseconds' is 60000.

(The cerr path is now per-seed, handed in by the caller: D1 moved every
 compile artefact under sandbox.vox's pid+seed scratch directory so two
 concurrently-running vox-fuzz processes can no longer overwrite each
 other's half-written source or cerr. Quoting it through 'shell quote'
 is belt-and-braces - the path is harness-generated (digits, underscores,
 slashes), never user input - but it keeps the one-audit-point property
 (plan 322 A2): every value that did not originate in this source reaches
 'run shell' through the same single-quoted helper.

 'the work dir' is the second half of D1's isolation. The vox compiler
 writes its intermediate `{stem}.asm` and `{stem}.o` to its OWN working
 directory (src/main.rs:637-638, `fs::write(&asm_path, ...)` with a
 bare relative name), not next to the source - so even with the source,
 binary and cerr each in their own per-seed scratch dir, two concurrent
 compiles of a same-stemmed source still collide on `vf_gen.asm` /
 `vf_gen.o` in the cwd, and the loser dies with `ld: cannot find
 vf_gen.o` / `NASM assembly failed` - a false asm-reject, exactly the
 defect D1 exists to close (hand-reproduced: the same source compiled
 solo exits 0, compiled 6x concurrently 5/6 fail on the intermediate).
 `cd`-ing the compile shell into the per-seed scratch dir first moves
 those intermediates inside it, so pid+seed isolation covers them too,
 and the per-seed sweep cleans them up along with everything else.
 source/out/cerr are leaf names relative to 'the work dir'; the unit
 tests pass "." so their fixture paths resolve exactly as before.)
(compile vox cds into the per-seed scratch dir so the compiler's
 intermediates land inside it (see the D1 note above). That cd breaks any
 vox_binary/vox_core path that was RELATIVE to the fuzzer's original cwd:
 the unit tests pass "." as the work dir (cd "." is a no-op, so a relative
 path still resolves), but the live gen loop passes the scratch path
 ./vf_scratch/run_{pid}_{seed}, and tests/110, 120, 130 and the confined
 runner all hand compile vox a relative --vox / default ../vox/... that no
 longer reaches the right file from inside the scratch dir. 'resolve from
 scratch' re-spells a relative path against the ORIGINAL cwd by walking up
 one directory per '/' in the work dir, so the path resolves from inside
 the scratch dir exactly as it did from the cwd. Absolute paths (leading
 '/') already resolve from anywhere and are returned unchanged.)
To 'resolve from scratch' with a text called path and a text called 'the work dir'.
    a buffer called 'the path bytes' is path.
    a number called 'the first byte' is byte 1 of 'the path bytes'.
    If 'the first byte' is 47 then,
        Return a text, path.
    a buffer called 'the dir bytes' is 'the work dir'.
    a number called 'the byte count' is 'the dir bytes's size.
    a number called 'the scan position' is 1.
    a buffer called 'the climb bytes'.
    While 'the scan position' is less than or equal to 'the byte count',
        a number called 'the current byte' is byte 'the scan position' of 'the dir bytes',
        If 'the current byte' is 47 then,
            append "../" to 'the climb bytes'.
        Set 'the scan position' to 'the scan position' add 1.
    a text called 'the climb prefix' is 'the climb bytes' as text.
    a text called 'the resolved path' is "{'the climb prefix'}{path}".
    Return a text, 'the resolved path'.

To 'compile vox' with a text called 'the work dir' and a text called source and a text called out and a text called 'the cerr path'.
    a text called 'the resolved vox' is 'resolve from scratch' of vox_binary and 'the work dir'.
    a text called 'the resolved core' is 'resolve from scratch' of vox_core and 'the work dir'.
    a text called 'the compile command' is "cd {'shell quote' of 'the work dir'} && VOX_CORE_PATH={'shell quote' of 'the resolved core'} {'shell quote' of 'the resolved vox'} {source} -o {out} > {'shell quote' of 'the cerr path'} 2>&1".
    a number called 'the compiler exit code' is 'run shell' of 'the compile command' and 'the compile budget in milliseconds'.
    Return a number, 'the compiler exit code'.

To 'run program' with a text called 'binary path' and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    a list called 'no arguments' is [].
    a text called 'the verdict' is supervise of 'binary path' and 'no arguments' and 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    Return a text, 'the verdict'.